// ============================================================================
// EXPRESSION RESOLUTION · CALLEE RESOLUTION
//
// One rule family per receiverKind, because the schema records the receiver's SHAPE and
// says explicitly that the shape is all we honestly know about a duck-typed receiver.
// Turning a shape into a target is this file's whole job.
//
// EVERY RULE HERE PRODUCES expr_call_candidate(Site, Method). Nothing here narrows —
// narrowing is resolution/dispatch.dl (the cap) and the tier assignment is
// call-edge-generation/call_chain.dl. Keeping candidate generation monotone and
// negation-free is what lets construction, dispatch and cls-flow be mutually recursive
// without breaking stratification.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE. If a receiver has no type, this file emits
// NOTHING. It does not fan to every method of that name in the program. On the measured
// corpus that fan is 24.63 candidates per attribute call and 1.22M candidate edges over
// 49k sites, and it has no soundness argument: two unrelated classes sharing a method
// name are not a dispatch set, they are a coincidence. ambiguous_unknown is the honest
// answer and the tier system is built to carry it.
// ============================================================================

// ── mro_lookup_demand(Prov, ReceiverType, Name) ──────────────────────────────
// The (type, name) pairs some call site actually asks about — bounds mro_lookup_miss to
// real questions instead of the cross product of every class and every name.
mro_lookup_demand("client", t, n) :- call_recv_type(site, t), call_name(site, n).

// ============================================================================
// (1) NO RECEIVER — `f(x)`. 30 of linkage-sample's 49 sites.
// ============================================================================

// ── a bare name that denotes a FUNCTION or METHOD ────────────────────────────
// Scope-correct by construction: the LEGB walk in resolution/name-resolution.dl already
// decided WHICH `register` this is, so a nested def and a module-level function of the
// same name cannot be confused. Covers module functions, imported functions, nested
// defs (helpers.build_pipeline's `register` / `normalise` / `summarise`), and the
// DECORATOR_CALL site of a parameterised decorator (`@audit("child")` -> models.audit).
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_denotes_method("client", callee, m).

// ── a bare name that denotes a CLASS — CONSTRUCTION ──────────────────────────
// `Base("b")`, `Sibling()`. Python construction is syntactically a call and the schema
// has no OBJECT_CREATION kind on purpose, so this is the join that decides it. The
// TARGET is __init__/__new__ FOUND THROUGH THE MRO — `Child("c")` has no __init__ of its
// own and constructs through Base.__init__, which is a real edge a name-only resolver
// never sees.
call_constructs_type(site, t) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_type_class_object("client", callee, t).
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :- call_constructs_type(site, t), type_constructor("client", t, m).

// ── `cls(...)` inside a classmethod — construction through the reached class ──
// The callee is a CLS_REFERENCE, so it is a no-receiver call whose callee denotes a
// class object; the clause above already covers it once expr_cls_type answers.

// ============================================================================
// (2) SELF — `self.m(x)`. Typed from the enclosing class, widened by dispatch.
// ============================================================================
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, "SELF"),
    call_recv_type(site, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    mro_lookup("client", t, n, m).

// ============================================================================
// (3) SUPER — an MRO-ordered lookup starting AFTER the anchor class.
//     NOT virtual dispatch. 7 of linkage-sample's 49 sites are the `super()` call
//     itself and 7 more are the method call on its result.
// ============================================================================

// ── call_super_anchor(SiteHash, ReceiverType, AnchorType) ────────────────────
// (a) ZERO-ARGUMENT `super()` — 98% of uses. CPython compiles it with an implicit
//     __class__ cell, so the anchor is the class the enclosing method is DEFINED IN,
//     which is exactly py_call_site.pyTypeLinkHash. The receiver type is
//     type(self), which is statically unknown; the standard approximation is the anchor
//     itself, and that is what the sample's ground truth expects.
// The two forms are separated by a PURELY STRUCTURAL test — does the inner `super`
// call have arguments — rather than by negating the resolved-anchor relation. That
// matters: call_super_explicit_anchor needs expr_type_class_object, which sits inside
// the resolution fixpoint, so negating it here would be cyclic negation and souffle
// refuses to stratify it. call_super_has_args reads only the AST.
call_super_has_args(site) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, "SUPER"),
    call_receiver_object(site, superCall),
    call_of_expr(superCall, superSite),
    call_arg(superSite, _, _).
// THE RECEIVER TYPE IS NOT THE ANCHOR, and 05-super exists to prove it. `super()`
// written in Left means "the class after Left in the MRO of TYPE(SELF)", and type(self)
// is not Left. With self a `Both`, MRO(Both) = [Both, Left, Right, Root] and the lookup
// after Left reaches Right.greet — a class that is not a base of Left and appears nowhere
// in Left's own bases. Any rule that reads super() as "my base class" gets this wrong,
// and this engine did until the receiver type was widened here: it answered Root.greet
// and missed Right.greet on both of that case's super sites.
//
// So the receiver type ranges over the same set as ordinary self-dispatch — the
// enclosing class plus every CONSTRUCTED subclass of it (resolution/dispatch.dl) —
// while the ANCHOR stays the class the method is defined in, which is what CPython's
// implicit __class__ cell holds.
call_super_anchor(site, t, k) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, "SUPER"),
    call_enclosing_type(site, k),
    !call_super_has_args(site),
    super_recv_type(site, t).

// ── super_recv_type(SiteKey, TypeHash) ───────────────────────────────────────
// (a) the enclosing class itself — always, so a class that is only ever reached through
//     another class's super() chain still resolves.
super_recv_type(site, k) :- call_recv_kind(site, "SUPER"), call_enclosing_type(site, k).
// (b) every CONSTRUCTED subclass of it.
super_recv_type(site, sub) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, "SUPER"), call_enclosing_type(site, k),
    type_subclass("client", k, sub), type_instantiated_reachable("client", sub).
// (b) EXPLICIT `super(A, self)` — still valid Python 3. The anchor is written as the
//     first ARGUMENT of the inner `super` call, so no extra schema column is needed
//     (the schema says so). The inner call is the receiver OBJECT of this site.
call_super_explicit_anchor(site, a) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, "SUPER"),
    call_receiver_object(site, superCall),
    call_of_expr(superCall, superSite),
    call_arg(superSite, "0", argExpr),
    expr_type_class_object("client", argExpr, a).
// The explicit form `super(Left, self)` names its own anchor, and the receiver still
// ranges over the constructed subclasses — the two forms must resolve identically, or
// the rule is keying on syntax rather than on the MRO.
call_super_anchor(site, t, a) :- call_super_explicit_anchor(site, a), super_recv_type(site, t).

// ── the super() target ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// mro_lookup_after searches only the part of the MRO strictly after the anchor, using
// the same prec-minimality argument as an ordinary lookup. This is what a set-valued
// ancestor relation cannot express: the anchor is a POSITION in an order, not a subtree.
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_super_anchor(site, t, anchor),
    call_name(site, n),
    mro_lookup_after("client", t, anchor, n, m).

// ============================================================================
// (4) NAME receiver — `Base.make_default()`, `Child.of("x")`, `mod.f()`, `obj.m()`
// ============================================================================

// ── the receiver names a CLASS: an unbound / static / class method ────────────
// `Child.of("x")` resolves through MRO to Base.of — inherited, and named on the
// SUBCLASS at the call site. That inheritance hop is the point.
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_type_class_object("client", obj, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    mro_lookup("client", t, n, m).

// ── the receiver names a MODULE: `mod.f()` ───────────────────────────────────
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_names_module("client", obj, mod),
    call_name(site, n),
    module_member_method("client", mod, n, m).

// ── a FUNCTION-VALUED ATTRIBUTE — `self.op(1, 2)` where `self.op = add` ───────
// The attribute holds a plain function, so NO `self` is bound and argument 0 lands on
// parameter 0. That is why type_attr_method_value is separate from mro_lookup: reading
// this as a method call would shift every argument by one.
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_recv_type(site, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    type_attr_method_value("client", t, n, m).

// ── A CALLABLE INSTANCE — `m(5)` where m is an object with __call__ ───────────
// The call site names the instance and never names __call__. Both receiver shapes
// occur: a bare name holding an instance (`m(5)`) and a construction called directly
// (`Delegator(target)(1, 2)`).
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_type("client", callee, t),
    type_call_target("client", t, m).
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    call_of_expr(callee, inner), call_constructs_type(inner, t),
    type_call_target("client", t, m).

// ── the receiver names a MODULE and the attribute is a CLASS: `mod.C()` ──────
call_constructs_type(site, t) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_names_module("client", obj, mod),
    call_name(site, n),
    module_member_type("client", mod, n, t).
// ── `Outer.Inner(...)` — CONSTRUCTION OF A NESTED CLASS ──────────────────────
// The same shape as the module clause above with a CLASS in the receiver position rather
// than a module. Without it the site is read as "call a method named Inner on Outer",
// Outer has no such method, and the site is `no_rule` — so the ONLY spelling Python gives
// a nested class resolved to nothing, while the bare name `Inner` resolved to every Inner
// in the module. Both halves are fixed together in resolution/name-resolution.dl.
//
// Two object shapes, because `A.B.C(...)` is one more hop: a bare class name, and an
// attribute chain that already resolved to a class. Neither reads
// expr_type_class_object — see the note on expr_attr_names_type for why that would not
// stratify.
call_constructs_type(site, t) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_names_type("client", obj, o),
    call_name(site, n),
    type_nested_member("client", o, n, t).
call_constructs_type(site, t) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_attr_names_type("client", obj, o),
    call_name(site, n),
    type_nested_member("client", o, n, t).

// ============================================================================
// (5) INSTANCE receivers — ATTRIBUTE, CALL_RESULT, NAME-holding-an-instance,
//     SUBSCRIPT. All go through the SAME rule, because once the receiver
//     expression has a type the shape stops mattering. That is the payoff of
//     typing the receiver EXPRESSION rather than special-casing each syntax.
// ============================================================================
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_recv_type(site, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    mro_lookup("client", t, n, m).

// ── the OVERRIDES, where the receiver is known only by declaration (#43) ─────
// One join against a relation computed entirely in the resolution layer.
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_recv_declared(site),
    call_recv_type(site, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    dispatch_override("client", t, n, m).

// ── a DECORATED method reached through an attribute ──────────────────────────
// `handlers.checkout(...)` where `checkout` is @retry-decorated: the name denotes the
// decorator's OUTPUT, so the target is `inner`, not the `def`. Same answer the bare-name
// path gets from expr_denotes_method; this is the attribute half of it.
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_recv_type(site, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    mro_lookup_decorated("client", t, n, m).

// ── call_recv_type(SiteHash, TypeHash) — the receiver's instance type(s) ─────
// (a) SELF: the enclosing class, plus the dispatch widening (resolution/dispatch.dl).
call_recv_type(site, t) :- call_self_dispatch_type(site, t).
// (b) any other receiver shape: the type of the receiver OBJECT expression.
//     `self.base.describe()`  -> field type of Holder.base   -> Base
//     `Sibling().describe()`  -> construction return type    -> Sibling
//     `d.describe()`          -> single-binding local flow
call_recv_type(site, t) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER", rk != "SELF",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_type("client", obj, t).

// ============================================================================
// (6) DECLARED UNKNOWNS — emitted as POSITIVE facts with a reason.
//     Conservation is not "we forgot to drop it"; it is a classification.
// ============================================================================

// ── call_unresolvable(SiteHash, Reason) ──────────────────────────────────────
// (a) dynamic by construction.
call_unresolvable(site, "dynamic_call") :- call_is_dynamic(site).
// (b) the callee is a bare name that resolves to a PARAMETER — the 68.2% shape.
//     `return fn(*args, **kwargs)` in models.audit.middle.wrapper is exactly this: `fn`
//     is a free variable bound to `middle`'s parameter, and the only sound statement
//     about it is that it is whatever the caller passed.
call_unresolvable(site, "callee_is_parameter") :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_names_param("client", callee, ph),
    !param_arg_method(ph, _), !param_arg_type(ph, _).
// (c) the receiver has no type the engine can derive.
call_unresolvable(site, cat("untyped_receiver:", reason)) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_type_untypable("client", obj, reason).
// (d) the MRO winner for the name is a decorator-replaced def.
call_unresolvable(site, "decorator_replaced_target") :-
    call_recv_type(site, t), call_name(site, n), mro_lookup_opaque("client", t, n).
// (e) the receiver type is known but nothing in its MRO defines the name, AND the
//     hierarchy has a hole (unresolved base, dynamic base, metaclass, __getattr__).
call_unresolvable(site, "escape_hatch") :-
    call_recv_type(site, t), call_name(site, n), mro_lookup_escape_hatch("client", t, n).
// (e2) the receiver's type is FULLY KNOWN and its MRO defines no such name. Disjoint
//      from escape_hatch above by construction: both read mro_lookup_miss, and
//      resolution/attribute-lookup.dl splits it on type_hierarchy_incomplete — a hole in
//      the hierarchy is an escape hatch, no hole is a genuine absence. The name is
//      therefore not a member of the analysed code at all: a C-level attribute, an
//      `object` member, or an attribute installed at runtime.
//
//      mro_lookup_absent was already derived, documented, and READ BY NOTHING — the same
//      shape as module_exports (#105) and param_kind's var-parameter kinds (#111). Its
//      absence here is what left these sites carrying `no_rule`, which
//      call-edge-generation/call_chain.dl defines as a site that could not be resolved
//      AND could not be explained.
call_unresolvable(site, "member_absent_from_type") :-
    call_recv_type(site, t), call_name(site, n), mro_lookup_absent("client", t, n).
// (f) the callee name resolved to nothing at all and is not a known builtin.
call_unresolvable(site, "unbound_name") :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_binding("client", b, _, callee),
    binding_lookup_unresolved("client", b, n, _),
    !py_builtin_callable(n).

// ============================================================================
// (7) BUILTIN BOUNDARY — the callee is known exactly, and is C code.
//     resolution/builtins.dl explains why this is boundary_lib and not a blind spot.
// ============================================================================

// ── call_hits_builtin(SiteHash, TargetLabel) ─────────────────────────────────
// (a) a bare name that resolved to no client declaration but IS a catalogued builtin.
//     `super()` x 7 on linkage-sample.
call_hits_builtin(site, label) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_binding("client", b, _, callee),
    binding_lookup_unresolved("client", b, n, _),
    builtin_target(n, label).
// (b) a construction of a class with no __init__ and no __new__ anywhere in its MRO:
//     the target is object.__init__, a C slot. `Sibling()` x 2 on linkage-sample.
call_hits_builtin(site, label) :-
    call_constructs_type(site, t),
    type_constructor_absent("client", t),
    !type_hierarchy_incomplete("client", t),
    builtin_object_init_target(label).

// (c) a super() call whose anchor's MRO leaves the IR at a NAMED BUILTIN base.
//     `class Installer(type)` then `super().__new__(...)`: `type` is a C-level class with
//     no Python source anywhere, so the lookup after Installer runs off the end of every
//     staged IR. The target IS nameable — type.__new__ — so this is a boundary, not the
//     blind spot it otherwise reads as.
call_hits_builtin(site, cat("builtin:", cat(baseText, cat(".", n)))) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, "SUPER"),
    call_super_anchor(site, _, anchor),
    call_name(site, n),
    !expr_call_candidate(site, _),
    type_base_unresolved("client", anchor, _, baseText),
    py_builtin_callable(baseText).

// (d) construction of a class whose member set is DYNAMICALLY EXTENDED — a monkey patch,
//     a metaclass, an exec-synthesised __init__ — and which defines no constructor the
//     engine can see. The target genuinely may not exist in this source (12-blind-spots'
//     `Synthesised` has no `def __init__` anywhere), so it is a declared unknown WITH A
//     REASON rather than a claimed edge to object.__init__.
call_unresolvable(site, "construction_of_dynamically_extended_class") :-
    call_constructs_type(site, t),
    type_constructor_absent("client", t),
    type_hierarchy_incomplete("client", t).

// ── call_hits_dynamic_builtin(SiteHash, Name) ────────────────────────────────
// getattr / eval / exec: not merely outside the IR — they make the program unanalysable
// at that point. Reported separately so "we cannot see the body" and "nobody can know
// what runs" are not the same row.
call_hits_dynamic_builtin(site, n) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_binding("client", b, _, callee),
    binding_lookup_unresolved("client", b, n, _),
    py_builtin_dynamic(n).

// ============================================================================
// CROSS-CHECK against the parser's own (run-local) answer
// ============================================================================

// ── callee_agrees / callee_differs / callee_engine_only / callee_parser_only ──
// py_call_site.resolvedCalleeHash is run-local: 39 of 49 sites on linkage-sample, all
// resolving within that one parse, and STRUCTURALLY incapable of matching a library
// hash. Comparing instead of consuming it means the engine's independence is measured,
// and a regression in either direction shows up as a diff rather than as a number that
// happens to stay the same.
callee_agrees(site, m) :- expr_call_candidate(site, m), csite(site, cs), call_pre_resolved("client", "METHOD", m, cs).
callee_agrees(site, m) :- expr_call_candidate(site, m), csite(site, cs), call_pre_resolved("client", "MODULE_FUNCTION", m, cs).
callee_differs(site, mine, theirs) :-
    expr_call_candidate(site, mine),
    csite(site, cs), call_pre_resolved("client", k, theirs, cs), theirs != "", theirs != mine,
    k != "TYPE",
    !expr_call_candidate(site, theirs).
callee_engine_only(site) :- expr_call_candidate(site, _), csite(site, cs), call_pre_resolved("client", "UNRESOLVED", _, cs).
callee_parser_only(site, theirs) :-
    csite(site, cs), call_pre_resolved("client", k, theirs, cs), theirs != "", k != "TYPE",
    !expr_call_candidate(site, _).

// ============================================================================
// THE LIBRARY HALF OF CALLEE RESOLUTION
// ============================================================================
// Same question as everything above -- WHICH callable does this site invoke -- for a
// site whose receiver or callee resolves into the staged library. It lives here, beside
// the client clauses, because this file owns callee resolution; it does not belong in
// the library file.
//
// STRUCTURAL REFERENCE, and the reason this moved: neither sibling engine puts call
// resolution in its library file. java/engine/resolution/lib-hierarchy.dl owns
// library-side FACTS (lib_type_parent, lib_method_return_type, lib_super_ref) and
// resolves calls in this file; typescript/engine/resolution/lib-scope.dl owns import and
// name binding only (lib_import_binds, lib_name_binds). Python's lib-linking.dl had
// grown a second method-lookup implementation -- MRO walks, callable-instance dispatch,
// constructor resolution -- duplicating what this file already does for the client.
//
// The MRO walk below is `mro_lookup("lib", ...)` -- the SAME relation the client path
// uses (resolution/mro.dl is prov-parametric), not a library-specific reimplementation.
// That is the property that keeps the two paths from disagreeing.

// ── call_constructs_lib_type(SiteHash, LibTypeHash) ──────────────────────────
call_constructs_lib_type(site, t) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_names_lib_type(callee, t).
call_constructs_lib_type(site, t) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_names_lib_module(obj, mod),
    call_name(site, n),
    module_member_type("lib", mod, n, t).

// ── call_recv_lib_type(SiteHash, LibTypeHash) ────────────────────────────────
// The receiver object denotes a library class (a class object being attribute-accessed,
// or a construction of one, or `mod.Class`).
call_recv_lib_type(site, t) :-
    call_receiver_object(site, obj), expr_names_lib_type(obj, t).
call_recv_lib_type(site, t) :-
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    call_of_expr(obj, inner), call_constructs_lib_type(inner, t).
call_recv_lib_type(site, t) :-
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_node("client", "ATTRIBUTE_ACCESS", _, n, obj),
    expr_parent("client", obj, "ATTRIBUTE_OBJECT", _, modExpr),
    expr_names_lib_module(modExpr, mod),
    module_member_type("lib", mod, n, t).

// ── call_hits_lib_method(SiteHash, LibMethodHash) ────────────────────────────
// (a) a bare-name call to an imported library function.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_names_lib_function(callee, m).
// (b) `mod.f()` where mod is an imported library module.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    expr_names_lib_module(obj, mod),
    call_name(site, n),
    module_member_method("lib", mod, n, m).
// (c) an attribute call whose receiver type is a LIBRARY class, resolved THROUGH THE
//     LIBRARY'S OWN MRO. That MRO comes from lib_py_type_base.resolvedTypeLinkHash,
//     which is a within-library-run FK and therefore valid (1,409 of 2,859 library base
//     rows carry one).
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_recv_lib_type(site, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    mro_lookup("lib", t, n, m).
// ── a CLIENT receiver whose MRO winner is a LIBRARY method ───────────────────
// `class Overrider(Mid)` over a library Mid: the receiver type is a CLIENT class, so
// the clause above (which keys on call_recv_lib_type) never applies, while the client
// path produces an expr_call_candidate that call-edge generation cannot use because the
// method is a library row. The linearisation, the definer set and the lookup were all
// correct by this point and the edge still did not exist.
// This is the ordinary framework-subclass shape: the client declares the class, the
// method it inherits lives in a dependency.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_recv_kind(site, rk), rk != "NONE", rk != "SUPER",
    call_recv_type(site, t),
    call_name(site, n),
    mro_lookup("client", t, n, m),
    method_decl("lib", _, _, _, _, m).
// (d) construction of a library class -> its __init__/__new__ through the library MRO.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :- call_constructs_lib_type(site, t), type_constructor("lib", t, m).

// ── a receiver whose EXPRESSION has a library type ───────────────────────────
// One clause over expr_lib_type_of, rather than one per shape. It subsumes the bare
// name, the self-attribute and the declared annotation; the construction and dotted
// forms stay separate above because they key off the site, not the receiver expression.
call_recv_lib_type(site, t) :-
    call_receiver_object(site, obj), expr_lib_type(obj, t).

// ── the chain: `IntBox().get().tag()` ────────────────────────────────────────
// The receiver of `.tag()` is the CALL `get()`, whose type generics.dl substituted.
call_recv_lib_type(site, t) :-
    call_receiver_object(site, obj),
    call_of_expr(obj, inner),
    call_returns_lib_type(inner, t).

// ============================================================================
// A CALLABLE INSTANCE OF A LIBRARY CLASS — `self.q_proj(x)`
// ============================================================================
//
// THE LARGEST UNRESOLVED SHAPE ON THE MEASURED CORPUS. On transformers, SELF_CALL
// is 32,455 sites at 30.5% resolved, and 22,505 of the 22,561 misses are this: an
// attribute holding a library instance, then called. `self.q_proj = nn.Linear(...)`
// followed by `self.q_proj(x)` invokes Linear.__call__ — the site never writes
// `__call__`, and `q_proj` is a FIELD, so method lookup on the owner finds nothing.
//
// expression-resolution/callee-resolution.dl already has both value-callee clauses,
// and resolution/attribute-lookup.dl derives type_call_target for EITHER provenance
// (`mro_lookup(p, t, "__call__", m)`, 2 rows on the torture corpus, both lib). Only
// the consumers were pinned to "client", so a library callable had no path. Same
// defect class as the re-export and generic-substitution fixes above.
//
// STOPS AT `__call__`. It does not chain on to nn.Module.__call__'s dispatch to
// `forward` — that is inside the library, engine-ii's problem, and LIB_BODY is
// staged empty so the statements are not even loaded.
//
// (a) a bare name holding a library instance: `c1 = Callable1(); c1(5)`.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, callee), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    binding_value_lib_type(b2, t),
    type_call_target("lib", t, m).
// (b) a construction called directly: `Callable1()(5)`.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_callee_is_value(site),
    call_callee_expr(site, callee),
    call_of_expr(callee, inner),
    call_constructs_lib_type(inner, t),
    type_call_target("lib", t, m).
// (c) `self.ATTR(x)` where ATTR is a field holding a library instance. The site is a
//     SELF_CALL naming the ATTRIBUTE, not a value-callee, so neither clause above can
//     reach it: call_receiver_object is bare `self` and call_name is the field name.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_name(site, n),
    call_self_dispatch_type(site, owner),
    type_attr_field("client", owner, n, f),
    field_lib_type(f, t),
    type_call_target("lib", t, m).
// the multi-write case: every write's type contributes, so the site gets the SOUND SET
// rather than one arbitrary member. resolution/field-flow.dl keeps the two apart.
call_hits_lib_method(site, m) :-
    call_name(site, n),
    call_self_dispatch_type(site, owner),
    type_attr_field("client", owner, n, f),
    field_lib_type_union(f, t),
    type_call_target("lib", t, m).

// ── THE CLIENT MIRROR of clause (c) ──────────────────────────────────────────
// `self.own_call(9)` where the attribute holds a CLIENT callable. The three clauses
// above were written for library types and this counterpart was never added, so an
// attribute holding a client instance with __call__ resolved while the identical
// library one did not -- the same one-provenance-only asymmetry this file has been
// converging away from, just pointing the other way.
// field_type is the client counterpart of field_lib_type (resolution/field-flow.dl)
// and type_call_target is already prov-parametric (resolution/attribute-lookup.dl).
expr_call_candidate(site, m) :-
    call_name(site, n),
    call_self_dispatch_type(site, owner),
    type_attr_field("client", owner, n, f),
    field_type("client", f, t),
    type_call_target("client", t, m).

// ── `returns_instance().name()` where the callee declares `-> Square` ────────
call_returns_lib_type(site, t) :-
    expr_call_candidate(site, m),
    method_declared_return_type_lib(m, d),
    declared_dispatch("lib", d, t).
